    \field{abstract}{%
    The evolution of intelligent manufacturing has brought new challenges to
  production scheduling, including human–machine resource coordination,
  operator skill diversity, and dynamic task changes. To address these issues,
  this paper proposes a dynamic scheduling framework with dual human–machine
  resource constraints, integrating graph neural networks and deep
  reinforcement learning to tackle task allocation and resource scheduling
  separately. A Multi-Behavior Graph Convolutional Network (MBGCN) is
  constructed to model factors such as operator skills, task complexity, and
  preferences, enabling high-quality Person-Job matching. Additionally, a
  Collective-Graph-Aware Double Deep Q Network (CGA-DDQN) is developed to
  enhance decision-making under dynamic conditions. Preliminary experiments
  demonstrate that the proposed approach achieves promising performance in task
  adaptability, scheduling responsiveness, and resource coordination,
  validating its potential in human–machine collaborative scheduling.%
    }
