  training, Domain adaptation, Work-related musculoskeletal disorder, Deep
  learning}
    \strng{namehash}{CWGD1}
    \strng{fullhash}{CWGD1}
    \field{labelnamesource}{author}
    \field{labeltitlesource}{title}
    \field{labelyear}{2025}
    \field{labeldatesource}{}
    \field{sortinit}{C}
    \field{sortinithash}{C}
    \field{abstract}{%
    Mitigating physical fatigue risks in the construction industry is crucial
  for ensuring workforce sustainability. Prior research has primarily employed
  computationally intensive computer vision models to assess workers' physical
  fatigue risks, achieving high accuracy but failing to enable real-time
  evaluation due to significant computational overhead. To address this
  challenge, this paper develops a teacher-student training paradigm-powered
  method that enables real-time physical fatigue risk assessment for
  construction workers without compromising accuracy. The proposed method
  leverages the teacher model's capabilities to generate high-quality learning
  materials, significantly improving the student model's 3D human pose
  estimation from 2D images, achieving a 3.8 % accuracy improvement on the
  MuPoTS benchmark dataset. Experimental findings reveal that the student model
  attains a mean average precision of 84.0 while maintaining real-time
  performance at 35 frames per second on consumer-level hardware with 6G GPU
  resources. Field testing demonstrates the effectiveness of the proposed
  method in real-world construction scenarios.%
    }
    \verb{doi}
    \verb https://doi.org/10.1016/j.autcon.2025.106372
    \endverb
    \field{issn}{0926-5805}
    \field{pages}{106372}
    \field{title}{Real-time physical fatigue risk assessment for construction
  workers using a teacher-student training paradigm}
    \verb{url}
    \verb https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0926580525004121
    \endverb
    \field{volume}{177}
    \field{journaltitle}{Automation in Construction}
    \field{year}{2025}
  \endentry

